"q": "An AWS Glue Spark job joins 2 billion order-line rows with a 300-million-row seller table on `seller_id`. Two marketplace sellers account for 45% of all order lines. In the Spark UI, all tasks of the join stage finish within minutes except a few that run for more than two hours. Adding more workers doesn't help.\n\nWhich approach will MOST effectively reduce the job's runtime?",
"options": [
"Call `coalesce()` on the order-line DataFrame before the join to reduce the number of partitions.",
"Salt the join key by adding a random suffix to the skewed sellers' keys and replicating the matching seller rows for each suffix.",
"Broadcast the order-line DataFrame to every executor so that the join no longer needs a shuffle.",
"Change the worker type from G.1X to G.4X and double the number of workers so that the long-running tasks get more memory and CPU."
],
"answer": [
1
],
"optExpl": [
"Fewer partitions concentrate the work even more.",
"Jawaban benar. Lihat pembahasan di atas.",
"You broadcast the *small* side of a join. Two billion rows won't fit in executor memory.",
"Bigger or more workers don't split a single partition's work. The hot tasks stay hot."
],
"explanation": "This is data skew. Every row for a hot key hashes to the same partition, so one task does most of the work no matter how many workers there are. Salting spreads a hot key across N sub-keys on the large side and replicates the matching rows on the small side, so the join still matches correctly while the work spreads over many tasks. (Spark's adaptive query execution skew-join handling can help in the same way, but salting is the reliable, explicit fix.)",
