"q": "An AWS Glue Spark job uses `spark.read.json()` to read an S3 prefix that holds about 2 million JSON files of roughly 10 KB each. The job fails with a driver `OutOfMemoryError` before any data is processed.\n\nWhich change will fix the failure with the LEAST effort?",
"options": [
"Change the worker type to a memory-optimized R.2X worker so that the driver has more memory to track file metadata during planning.",
"Double the number of workers so that more executors share the work of reading the files.",
"Increase the job timeout, and turn on continuous logging to capture the driver's memory usage.",
"Read the files with a Glue DynamicFrame that sets `groupFiles` to `inPartition` with a `groupSize`, and use `useS3ListImplementation`."
],
"answer": [
3
],
"optExpl": [
"A bigger driver only delays the failure as the file count grows, and it costs more on every run.",
"This is a driver problem. More executors don't reduce driver memory use.",
"The job fails from memory pressure, not from running out of time.",
"Jawaban benar. Lihat pembahasan di atas."
],
"explanation": "Spark's file reader tracks every file on the driver and creates a task for each small file, so millions of tiny files exhaust driver memory. Glue's S3 file grouping combines many files into each task, which Glue turns on automatically above 50,000 files for DynamicFrame reads. `useS3ListImplementation` lists S3 in batches, which reduces driver memory further.",
