"q": "An AWS Glue Spark job joins a 3 TB `events` table with a 2 TB `sessions` table on `session_key`. One key value, used for anonymous traffic, accounts for about 40% of the rows. The Spark UI shows one task running for hours while the others finish in minutes. The job sets `spark.sql.adaptive.enabled` to `false`, left over from an old tuning exercise. Which actions will reduce the impact of the skew? (Select TWO.)",
"options": [
"Repartition both DataFrames on `session_key` before the join so that the rows are spread across more partitions.",
"Add more workers to the job and raise `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` so that the long-running task gets more executor capacity and finishes sooner.",
"Salt the skewed key by adding a random suffix on the `events` side, copying the matching `sessions` rows once for each suffix, and joining on the salted key.",
"Add a broadcast hint for the `sessions` table so that the join runs as a broadcast hash join.",
"Turn on adaptive query execution with skew join handling so that Spark splits oversized partitions at run time."
],
"answer": [
2,
4
],
"optExpl": [
"Repartitioning on the skewed key sends every row with that key to the same partition again.",
"One task runs in one executor, so more workers leave the hot partition as large as before.",
"Jawaban benar. Lihat pembahasan di atas.",
"A 2 TB table is far too large to broadcast to every executor.",
"Jawaban benar. Lihat pembahasan di atas."
],
"explanation": "Salting spreads one hot key across many partitions, so the work is shared by many tasks. Adaptive query execution (AQE) with skew join handling detects oversized shuffle partitions during a sort-merge join and splits them automatically. Both are standard fixes for data skew.",
"hint": "Kata kunci di soal: \"about 40% of the rows\", \"one task running for hours\"",
