"type": "single",
"q": "A retail company streams order events through Amazon Data Firehose into an Apache Iceberg table in an S3 general-purpose bucket. The table is registered in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Dashboards in Amazon Athena must show new orders within 5 minutes. After six weeks, Athena queries against the table have become slow. Investigation shows tens of thousands of small data files, many position-delete files from Firehose updates, and thousands of snapshots. The data must stay in the current bucket because existing S3 replication and lifecycle rules depend on it.\n\nWhich solution will fix the performance problem with the LEAST operational overhead?",
"options": [
"Create an Amazon EventBridge Scheduler schedule that starts an AWS Step Functions workflow every night. The workflow runs Athena `OPTIMIZE ... REWRITE DATA USING BIN_PACK` and then `VACUUM` against the table.",
"Raise the Firehose buffering hints to 128 MiB and 900 seconds so that Firehose commits fewer, larger files to the table.",
"Enable the AWS Glue Data Catalog table optimizers for compaction, snapshot retention, and orphan file deletion on the table.",
"Create an Amazon S3 table bucket, migrate the table into it with an Athena CTAS statement, and point the Firehose stream at the new table."
],
"answer": [
2
],
"optExpl": [
"It works, but you build and operate a scheduler, a workflow, IAM permissions and error handling. That's more overhead than turning on a managed optimizer.",
"A 900-second buffer breaks the 5-minute freshness requirement. It also does nothing about the files, delete files and snapshots that already exist.",
"Jawaban benar. Lihat pembahasan di atas.",
"S3 Tables would maintain the table automatically, but the stem says the data must stay in the current bucket. A migration is also more work than a configuration change."
],
"explanation": "Firehose commits files and snapshots but never compacts or expires them on a self-managed Iceberg table. Glue Data Catalog table optimizers are the managed way to run the three maintenance tasks on Iceberg tables in general-purpose buckets. Compaction merges small files and applies the delete files, snapshot retention expires old snapshots, and orphan file deletion removes unreferenced files. It's a per-table setting with no jobs or schedules to maintain.",
"hint": "Kata kunci di soal: \"small data files\", \"position-delete files\", \"must stay in the current bucket\", \"LEAST operational overhead\"",
