"q": "An insurance company built an Amazon Bedrock knowledge base over 3,000 policy manuals in Amazon S3. The vector store is an Amazon OpenSearch Serverless vector collection. The data source uses fixed-size chunking of 300 tokens. Users report that the retrieved chunks match their questions precisely, but the answers are often incomplete because the surrounding clauses of the policy are missing.\n\nWhich change will MOST effectively fix this problem?",
"options": [
"Update the existing data source's chunking configuration to hierarchical chunking, and then start a sync so that only the changed documents are re-chunked.",
"Create a new data source for the same S3 location with hierarchical chunking, run a sync to ingest it, and then remove the original data source.",
"Recreate the data source with no chunking so that each manual is embedded as a single chunk and the model always receives the full document.",
"Increase the number of results that each `RetrieveAndGenerate` call returns from 5 to 50 so that more chunks reach the model."
],
"answer": [
1
],
"optExpl": [
"You can't change the chunking strategy of an existing data source. A sync also only reprocesses new, changed or deleted documents, not the whole corpus.",
"Jawaban benar. Lihat pembahasan di atas.",
"One vector for a whole manual blurs matching badly. Long manuals also go past the embedding model's input limit (8,192 tokens for Titan Text Embeddings V2), and you lose page-level citations.",
"More results adds noisy, loosely related chunks and more tokens. The chunks still don't contain the clauses around each match."
],
"explanation": "**Hierarchical chunking** searches small **child** chunks for precise matches and returns their larger **parent** chunks, so the model gets the surrounding context. That's the exact fix for \"precise but incomplete.\" The chunking strategy is fixed when a data source is created, so you need a new data source and a re-ingestion. The OpenSearch Serverless store is a good fit. Hierarchical chunking isn't recommended with S3 Vectors because of its per-vector metadata limits, but that isn't the store here.",
