"q": "A payments company is building a real-time fraud-scoring API. Each request must find the most similar past transactions by vector similarity in single-digit milliseconds, with recall above 99%. The same service must also read and write per-customer session data as key/value pairs with microsecond read latency. All data must survive a node failure without loss, and the team wants to operate a single data store.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
"options": [
"Store the transaction embeddings in an Amazon S3 Vectors index and the session data in an Amazon DynamoDB table.",
"Deploy an Amazon ElastiCache (Valkey) cluster as the only data store for both the vectors and the session data, and take automatic daily snapshots to protect the data.",
"Create an Amazon MemoryDB cluster with vector search enabled at creation, build an HNSW index for the embeddings, and store the session data as key/value pairs in the same cluster.",
"Store the embeddings in Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL with a pgvector HNSW index, and put an Amazon ElastiCache cluster in front of Aurora for the session data."
],
"answer": [
2
],
"optExpl": [
"S3 Vectors is built for low cost at sub-second latency (about 100 ms for frequent queries), not single-digit milliseconds. It also means running two stores.",
"ElastiCache is a cache. Daily snapshots lose every write since the last snapshot, so it doesn't meet \"survive a node failure without loss\" as the only data store.",
"Jawaban benar. Lihat pembahasan di atas.",
"Aurora with pgvector is a good vector store, but this design runs two services, and the cache layer has the same durability gap as B."
],
"explanation": "MemoryDB is an in-memory database that is also **durable**. Every write goes to a Multi-AZ transaction log before it's acknowledged, so it can be the system of record. Its vector search (FLAT and HNSW) returns results in single-digit milliseconds at more than 99% recall, and key/value reads take microseconds. Vector search has to be turned on when the cluster is created and runs on a single shard.",
