"q": "A legal-tech company stores 5 million document embeddings in an Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL table with the pgvector extension. A data engineer created the table and immediately ran `CREATE INDEX ON docs USING ivfflat (embedding vector_cosine_ops) WITH (lists = 100)`, and then bulk-loaded all 5 million rows. Similarity queries with the `<=>` operator are fast, but they often miss documents that exact search returns.\n\nWhich combination of actions will improve recall? (Select TWO.)",
"options": [
"Drop the IVFFlat index and create it again now that the table is loaded, with `lists` set to roughly the square root of the row count (about 2,200).",
"Run `SET hnsw.ef_search = 200` in the session before the similarity queries so that the index search examines more candidates.",
"Increase `ivfflat.probes` from its default of 1, starting at roughly the square root of `lists`, so that each query searches more clusters.",
"Rebuild the index with the `vector_ip_ops` operator class, and change the queries to use `<#>` because inner product returns more accurate neighbors.",
"Move the cluster to instances with Aurora Optimized Reads so that the index can be read from local NVMe storage."
],
"answer": [
0,
2
],
"optExpl": [
"Jawaban benar. Lihat pembahasan di atas.",
"`hnsw.ef_search` only affects HNSW indexes. It does nothing for IVFFlat.",
"Jawaban benar. Lihat pembahasan di atas.",
"The operator has to match the metric the embeddings were built for. Inner product isn't \"more accurate,\" and with normalized vectors it gives the same ranking as cosine anyway. It doesn't fix the stale centroids.",
"Optimized Reads can speed up queries when the index is bigger than memory. It doesn't change which neighbors the index returns."
],
"explanation": "IVFFlat learns its cluster centroids with k-means **when the index is built**. The centroids from an empty table describe nothing, so vectors end up in poorly chosen clusters. Rebuild after loading, with `lists` ≈ √rows once you're past about 1 million rows (**A**). At query time, `ivfflat.probes` controls how many clusters are searched. The default of 1 is fast but gives poor recall, and √lists is the usual starting point (**C**). Switching to HNSW, which needs no training, would also work, but it isn't offered here.",
