"q": "A research company runs semantic search on Amazon OpenSearch Service over 1 billion 768-dimension embeddings. The index uses HNSW with the Faiss engine and float32 vectors, and it needs so many memory-optimized nodes that the cost is over budget. The company must stay on OpenSearch because the application also uses keyword search and aggregations. A modest drop in recall is acceptable.\n\nWhich change will reduce the index's memory requirement the MOST?",
"options": [
"Increase the HNSW `m` parameter from 16 to 32 so that the graph finds good neighbors while visiting fewer nodes, and fewer nodes need to be cached in memory per query.",
"Rebuild the index as an IVF-PQ index (IVF with product quantization) on the Faiss engine, trained first with the Train API.",
"Keep the HNSW index, and enable Faiss scalar quantization to store the vectors as fp16 instead of float32.",
"Keep the HNSW index, and switch it from the Faiss engine to the Lucene engine with the same `m` and `ef_construction` values."
],
"answer": [
1
],
"optExpl": [
"A higher `m` adds graph links per node, which **increases** memory (the estimate is about `1.1 × (4·d + 8·m)` bytes per vector).",
"Jawaban benar. Lihat pembahasan di atas.",
"fp16 scalar quantization does reduce memory, but only by about half. It's a valid saving, just much smaller than PQ's.",
"Lucene HNSW stores the same float32 vectors and graph links, so memory stays roughly the same."
],
"explanation": "Product quantization splits each vector into sub-vectors and stores a short code for each, which compresses vectors far more than any other option here. IVF adds cluster-based search on top. In an AWS benchmark, IVF-PQ used about **16× less memory than HNSW**, in exchange for lower recall and higher latency, which the stem accepts. IVF-PQ has to be trained first.",
