"skill": "2.1.7",
"type": "single",
"q": "A subscription business uses AWS DMS to write change data capture (CDC) files from its customer database to Amazon S3 every hour. Each record has an `op` column with the value I, U, or D. The files are registered in the AWS Glue Data Catalog as a staging table. Analysts query an Apache Iceberg `customers` table in Amazon Athena, and it must always show the current state of each customer, with ACID guarantees and as little custom code as possible.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
"options": [
"Run an hourly AWS Glue job that reads the full `customers` table and the new staging files, removes duplicates in Spark, and overwrites the whole table as plain Parquet files.",
"Convert the `customers` table to Apache Hudi on an Amazon EMR cluster, and run a Spark Structured Streaming job that upserts the CDC records by using the customer ID as the record key and the change timestamp as the precombine field.",
"Schedule an hourly Athena `MERGE INTO customers` statement whose source is a view of each customer's latest change, and delete, update, or insert rows based on `op`.",
"Convert the `customers` table to an AWS Lake Formation governed table, and use its automatic ACID transactions to apply the staging records."
],
"answer": [
2
],
"optExpl": [
"Rewriting the whole table every hour is expensive, and plain Parquet has no ACID. Readers can see partial overwrites.",
"Hudi works for upserts, but it means changing the table format and running EMR. \"Upsert\" alone doesn't make Hudi the answer when Iceberg `MERGE` already fits.",
"Jawaban benar. Lihat pembahasan di atas.",
"Lake Formation governed tables were discontinued at the end of 2024. It's a distractor, and AWS points customers to Iceberg."
],
"explanation": "Athena supports `MERGE INTO` on Iceberg tables. One statement handles all three operations: `WHEN MATCHED AND s.op = 'D' THEN DELETE`, `WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE`, and `WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT`. Iceberg commits it atomically. The source view keeps only the latest change per key, because `MERGE` fails if several source rows match the same target row. It's plain SQL on a schedule, with no cluster.",
"hint": "Kata kunci di soal: \"CDC … op column I, U, or D\", \"Iceberg\", \"ACID\", \"as little custom code as possible\"",
